  "response": "Prevailing medical opinion in the 1970s attributed peptic ulcers to stress and excess gastric acid secretion. That attribution resulted directly from the fact that the stomach was widely believed to be too acidic for bacteria to colonize: since no organism was thought capable of surviving in gastric juice, the cause of ulceration had to be sought in secretory physiology and in the patient's nervous state, and therapy was aimed accordingly at neutralizing or suppressing acid.\n\nThe first crack in that framework was histological. Robin Warren observed curved bacteria in gastric biopsy specimens at Royal Perth Hospital in 1979. Only afterwards, in 1982, was the bacterium first successfully cultured, the breakthrough arriving when culture plates were left to incubate for five days over an Easter holiday. That success in the laboratory led Warren and Marshall to publish letters in The Lancet in 1983 describing unidentified curved bacilli in the human stomach.\n\nThe organism's persistence in so hostile an environment has a straightforward biochemical explanation: Helicobacter pylori produces urease and generates ammonia that buffers the surrounding acid. This mechanism cannot be squared with the older conviction that gastric acidity precluded bacterial colonization altogether; the two pictures of the gastric niche cannot both be correct, and the urease chemistry is what rules the earlier one out. Barry Marshall drank a broth culture of the bacterium in 1984 to test its ability to cause disease, and that deliberate ingestion caused a bleeding duodenal ulcer, which was documented at endoscopy.\n\nWhat finally settled the dispute, however, was therapeutic rather than microbiological. Duodenal ulcers treated with acid suppression alone relapsed in most patients within a year of stopping the drug; the outcomes obtained once antibacterial treatment was added ran in precisely the opposite direction. Randomized controlled trials showed that antibiotic eradication of the infection cut duodenal ulcer recurrence to a few percent, a finding that confirms that eradicating the infection with a course of antibiotics cured ulcer disease and prevented relapse. That is to say, clearing the bacterium with antimicrobial therapy produced lasting remission of peptic ulcers. This pattern of cure, reproduced across trial populations, converted a contested hypothesis into standard gastroenterological practice, and institutional recognition followed only after that therapeutic result was firmly on record. The Nobel Assembly at Karolinska Institutet determined that the 2005 prize in Physiology or Medicine be divided equally between Robin Warren and Barry Marshall, a decision that leaves no room for the notion that the pair received the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 2001, since a prize awarded in 2005 cannot have been handed to them four years earlier.",
